---
/*
  ConstellationSuite — a constellation, landed inside (the Sky's core surface).

  Typeset, not carded (decided 2026-07-23 after the sky-lab bench): each medium in its
  own register at its natural size. The margin carries the actual figure — a
  star at every fragment, joined by a drawn line — one continuous spline through
  the marks, interrupted by a gap at each so the stars sit in its gaps rather
  than on it. (It was per-segment alternating arcs until 2026-08-11, which put a
  26–46° corner at every star and a fixed periodic wave down every phone; see
  `lib/sky-figure.ts` for the whole account.)

  TWO lights answer the same reading line as you scroll (app.css "The Sky"):
  the stanza lamplight, a spotlight on the single nearest fragment; and the
  stars, on a far wider smoothstep falloff, so a dozen are alight at once and
  the figure kindles ahead of you and settles behind.

  ⚠ THE LAMPLIGHT IS ATMOSPHERE, NOT AN AFFORDANCE, and for a while it was
  quietly pretending to be one. It warms a quote's blockquote and an essay's
  title with the same gesture, so on a phone — where hover, the only thing that
  ever distinguished them, does not exist — the one dynamic thing on screen was
  teaching "everything here glows". A reader could not tell which stanzas open.

  The fix was not to take the light away. It was to give one KIND of stanza a
  word for it to warm (`suite-cta`, writing only): the light then stops being
  the signal and becomes the carrier of one, and a quote still glows but has
  nothing to say.

  ⚠ AND THE TEST IS NOT "DOES IT OPEN?" — quotes open too, as of plan 32. It is
  **is the stanza incomplete without the click?** An essay here is a title and a
  fading excerpt; the piece is elsewhere, and a reader who does not click has
  not read it. A quote is set whole, and clicking only adds provenance and a
  share mark to something already finished. A word there would promise text that
  is not missing. (Written down because the looser reading was built first, and
  is the one a later session would arrive at again.) `.is-focus` — scripts/focus-mode.ts — is what
  warms it, which is the lamplight on touch and the cursor on a desktop, never
  both at once.

  Each stanza's own register lives in `SuiteStanza.astro` — quotes set whole,
  writing as title + fading excerpt opening the blog's Reader, songs when they
  land — shared verbatim with the composer's Read view so the workshop shows
  the real thing. What stays HERE is this surface's chrome: the margin marks,
  the stanza offsets, the drawn line, the lamplight and the zoom.

  ⚠ A WRITING STANZA SITS ON A PAGE, and the two elements below are what carry
  it: `.suite-writing` on the article and the `.grow` text column inside it. The
  sheet is CSS (admin.css "THE PAGE", ADR-0029), keyed on exactly that pair — so
  it is not this component's to draw, but it IS this component's to keep. Rename
  either, or move the `Read →` out of `.grow`, and the page silently stops being
  one. The left edge of that sheet is deliberately absent so the drawn line
  passes through it rather than being interrupted; the figure and the paper are
  therefore no longer independent, and changing `INDENTS` moves both. (Since
  2026-08-11 that coupling is tighter still: the offsets are now the ONLY thing
  shaping the line, so a stanza that moves redraws the constellation.)

  The zoom out is navigation: the name itself (popover semantics — the trigger
  that opened it closes it), the ✦ beside it, the floating ✦ once you scroll,
  and Escape all go home — `/` IS the whole sky (the overview) — and the title
  morphs there via its transition:name, shared with the overview list. The
  suite ends by resolving UPWARD — never a sideways hop — restoring
  perspective. ("Sky" stays internal design vocabulary; the visible label is
  the plainest word available, see the outro.)
*/
import Reader from './Reader.astro';
import PostArticle from './PostArticle.astro';
import QuoteArticle from './QuoteArticle.astro';
import SuiteStanza from './SuiteStanza.astro';
import Label from './Label.astro';
import type { Constellation, SuiteItem } from '../lib/constellations';
import type { WritingItem } from '../lib/blog';
import { embedForUrl } from '../lib/media';
import { renderMarkdown } from '../lib/markdown';
import StarBloom from './StarBloom.astro';

interface Props {
  constellation: Constellation;
}
const { constellation } = Astro.props;

/*
  Asymmetric offsets — the drawn line gets its angles from these, and that is
  the whole reason they exist: the figure's shape is meant to be a consequence
  of where the stanzas sit, not a decoration laid over them.

  ⚠ NARROW GETS ITS OWN SET, AND WITHOUT IT THE PHONE FIGURE IS A RULER. Until
  2026-08-11 the offsets were `sm:` only, so on a phone every mark shared an x
  and the marks were COLLINEAR — which left the drawn line nothing to take its
  angles from. What actually curved it there was the old arcs builder's ±30px
  alternating bow, clamped to the same amplitude on every segment: a periodic
  sine wave down the margin, identical for every constellation, unrelated to
  anything you were reading. So design.md §235's "on phones it degrades to a
  near-vertical thread" described the most decorative figure on the site.
  Removing the bow without adding these would have swapped one wrong answer for
  another — a spline through collinear marks is dead straight.

  A third of the desktop amplitude: enough for the line to drift and lean, small
  enough that the prose does not visibly stagger. Max excursion on a 390px
  screen is ~23px.
*/
const INDENTS = ['0%', '9%', '3%', '15%', '0%', '11%', '5%', '13%'];
const NARROW_INDENTS = ['0%', '3%', '1%', '5%', '0%', '4%', '2%', '4%'];

const writings = constellation.items
  .filter((i): i is Extract<SuiteItem, { kind: 'writing' }> => i.kind === 'writing')
  .map((i) => i.item as WritingItem);

/*
  The score — a playlist for the whole read, rendered only when set.

  ⚠ `embedForUrl`, NOT `parseSpotifyEmbed` + a hand-written frame (plan 42 · §5).
  This iframe was assembled by hand and got two of its three attributes wrong,
  both silently:

   · `height="152"` is the TRACK height. A score is a PLAYLIST, whose frame is
     352 — so the player showed about two rows of a playlist that might hold
     twenty, on the public page, since the day it shipped.
   · `allow="encrypted-media"` alone is the exact configuration `lib/media.ts`
     documents as the cause of Michael's *"some embeds play button is broken...
     I can't reproduce it reliably"* on 2026-08-10. That fix added `autoplay`
     to `SPOTIFY_ALLOW` and never reached this file, because this file did not
     read from it.

  `embedForUrl` returns `{ src, height, allow }` TOGETHER, which is the whole
  point: three call sites each holding their own copy of those three facts is
  what produced two wrong ones. It also widens what a score may be — the action
  validates `score_url` with `new URL()` and nothing more, so a YouTube link is
  storable today and used to render NOTHING here (`parseSpotifyEmbed` returned
  null and the block vanished). Hence the two branches below.
*/
const score = constellation.scoreUrl ? embedForUrl(constellation.scoreUrl) : null;
---

<section class:list={['mx-auto max-w-4xl px-5 py-14 sm:px-8 sm:py-20', `cn-${constellation.color}`]}>
  {
    constellation.status === 'draft' && (
      <Label variant="chrome" class="mb-4">
        draft — visible only to you
      </Label>
    )
  }
  {
    /* `group` spans the mark AND the name: both go home, so hovering either
      blooms the star. (The bloom IS the hover feedback now — the old opacity
      dip would only double-dim it.) */
  }
  <div class="group flex items-baseline gap-3">
    <a
      href="/"
      class="sky-star"
      style="--glow: 1"
      transition:name={`star-${constellation.slug}`}
      aria-label="All constellations"
      title="All constellations"><StarBloom active /></a
    >
    {
      /* The name is the same trigger that opened it — tap again to close
        (zoom out), popover semantics at page scale. */
    }
    <h1 class="font-display text-4xl leading-[1.05] font-light sm:text-5xl" transition:name={`c-${constellation.slug}`}>
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-primary transition-colors" title="All constellations">
        {constellation.name}
      </a>
    </h1>
  </div>
  {
    constellation.description && (
      /* The epigraph. Markdown since 2026-08-11 — a <div> rather than a <p>
         because a rendered description may be more than one paragraph, and
         `.cn-desc` is what gives those paragraphs their space back (app.css,
         which also explains why this text does NOT wear `.reading`). The
         `italic` here is what `.cn-desc.italic em` keys off, so emphasis inside
         an already-italic epigraph turns upright instead of vanishing. */
      <div
        class="cn-desc prose-measure border-base-300 text-base-content/55 mt-5 border-l pl-5 text-lg italic"
        set:html={renderMarkdown(constellation.description)}
      />
    )
  }

  {
    score && (
      <div class="mt-9 max-w-xl">
        {/* Two shapes, the same split `PostArticle` already makes for a paired
            song: `height === null` means the frame is 16:9 video and wants an
            aspect box, anything else is a fixed-height player row. */}
        {score.height === null ? (
          <div class="aspect-video overflow-hidden" style="border-radius: var(--radius-box)">
            <iframe
              class="h-full w-full"
              src={score.src}
              loading="lazy"
              allow={score.allow}
              title={`The score — ${constellation.name}`}
              style="border: 0"
            />
          </div>
        ) : (
          <iframe
            src={score.src}
            width="100%"
            height={score.height}
            loading="lazy"
            allow={score.allow}
            title={`The score — ${constellation.name}`}
            style="border-radius: var(--radius-box); border: 0"
          />
        )}
        <p class="text-base-content/40 mt-2 font-sans text-xs">the score — press play, then read</p>
      </div>
    )
  }

  <div id="suite" class="suite text-base-content mt-16 flex flex-col gap-16">
    {
      constellation.items.map((f, i) => (
        <article
          class:list={['suite-item flex gap-4 sm:gap-6', f.kind === 'writing' && 'suite-writing']}
          style={`--indent:${INDENTS[i % INDENTS.length]};--indent-narrow:${NARROW_INDENTS[i % NARROW_INDENTS.length]}`}
        >
          {/* The margin column. It carries NO type glyph any more (2026-08-07):
              ” ♪ ▤ answered a question nobody asked — a reader can see that a
              quote is a quote — while spending the one piece of margin the star
              needs, and read as an unexplained symbol rather than as meaning.
              The star says "a fragment lives here", in the site's own
              vocabulary, and says it better alone.

              The span STAYS, empty. It is the gutter that holds the text column
              off the margin AND the box `drawLine()` measures each joint from.
              (Measured before removing the glyph: this box stretches to the
              stanza's height either way, so the figure did not move a pixel.)

              ⚠ `mt-1.5` went WITH the glyph, and had to. Those 6px existed to
              drop the glyph onto the first line's baseline — but the box they
              offset is the one every star is centred in, so with nothing left
              to align they were just pushing all eleven stars 3px below their
              stanzas' centres, for a reason that had been deleted. */}
          <span class="suite-mark w-5 shrink-0 select-none" aria-hidden="true" />
          <div class="min-w-0 grow">
            <SuiteStanza item={f} />
            {
              /* The invitation, on writing only — and that is the entire point.
                The lamplight passes over every stanza alike, which is what made
                it a liar: a warming quote and a warming essay said the same
                thing. Give ONLY the openable ones a word for the light to warm
                and the light stops being the signal and becomes the carrier of
                one. The quote still glows; it simply has nothing to say.

                aria-hidden + tabindex="-1": this duplicates the stanza's own
                link, which already has the accessible name and the tab stop. */
              /* ⚠ STILL WRITING ONLY, and a quote opening does NOT change that
                — which is the correction, because for one commit it did. A
                quote briefly carried an `About →` here on the reasoning that
                the rule was "openable stanzas get a word". That was the wrong
                reading of the rule. Michael, 2026-08-10:

                  "quotes dont need the about button. they should just be
                   clickable … it's not ultimately necessary to click the
                   quotes. It's only really meant for sharing or viewing
                   related content. However, we do need the read a button for
                   my writing because you obviously need to click it to read
                   the whole thing."

                So the rule is sharper than "openable": **a word belongs to a
                stanza whose content is INCOMPLETE WITHOUT THE CLICK.** An essay
                here is a title and a fading excerpt — the piece is elsewhere,
                and a reader who does not click has not read it. A quote is set
                whole; clicking adds provenance and a share mark to something
                already finished. A word on that promises the reader they are
                missing text, and they are not.

                It costs discoverability, knowingly — the same price and the
                same argument QuoteReveal's header records for the citation
                control: an answer to a question most readers never ask, reached
                by the ones who do ask because they reach for the line anyway. */
              f.kind === 'writing' && (
                <a
                  href={`/blog/${f.item.slug}`}
                  data-read={f.item.slug}
                  class="suite-cta"
                  tabindex="-1"
                  aria-hidden="true"
                >
                  <span class="suite-cta__word">Read</span>
                  <span class="suite-cta__arrow">→</span>
                </a>
              )
            }
          </div>
        </article>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  {
    /* The outro always resolves UPWARD to the overview, never sideways. Plain
      "return" on purpose: "all constellations" read as a breadcrumb in a
      catalogue, but anything more evocative than back/return makes a visitor
      hesitate over what it will do — the one place on this site where being
      ordinary is the point. The arrow leans the way you're about to go, and
      the warm accent is held for hover so a 4xl line isn't burning at rest.
      (Its generous height is also scroll runway for the lamplight's hand-off.) */
  }
  <div class="border-base-300 mt-24 border-t pt-16 pb-10 sm:pt-24">
    <a
      href="/"
      class="group return-link font-display hover:text-primary inline-flex items-center gap-3 text-3xl font-light transition-colors sm:text-4xl"
    >
      <svg
        class="return-arrow"
        viewBox="0 0 24 24"
        aria-hidden="true"
        fill="none"
        stroke="currentColor"
        stroke-width="1.5"
        stroke-linecap="round"
        stroke-linejoin="round"
      >
        <path d="M20 12H4"></path>
        <path d="m10.5 5.5-6.5 6.5 6.5 6.5"></path>
      </svg>
      return
    </a>
  </div>
</section>

{/* The blog's Reader, verbatim — a fragment reads identically everywhere. */}
<Reader />
{
  writings.map((w) => (
    <template data-reader-content={w.slug}>
      <PostArticle item={w} linkTitle />
    </template>
  ))
}
{
  /* And the quotes (plan 32). `inConstellation` drops THIS constellation from
    the sheet's "Appears in" — a door back into the room you are standing in is
    not a door, and counting it would make the control promise one more place
    than it can take you. */
  constellation.items
    .filter((f) => f.kind === 'quote')
    .map((f) => {
      const page = constellation.neighbourhoods.get(f.quote.id);
      return page ? (
        <template data-reader-content={f.quote.slug}>
          <QuoteArticle page={page} inConstellation={constellation.slug} />
        </template>
      ) : null;
    })
}

<a href="/" class="group sky-return" data-sky-return aria-label="All constellations" title="All constellations"
  ><StarBloom /></a
>

<script>
  import '../scripts/constellation-suite';
</script>
